
A context read written inside a slot population belongs to the caller, so the
context lowering has to see it there. It did not: the reachability scan, the
call-edge scan and the provider threading each walked a NodeInst's children
and skipped its Slots, so the enclosing component was never marked as reading
the context, no hidden parameter was threaded to it, and the *ir.ContextRead
survived lowering to reach codegen -- where irwalk.EvalExpr has no case for
one and every platform panicked.

This is the plain case: no provider anywhere, so the value each target has to
carry into the slot body is the context's own default. Three targets, because
the panic was every platform's.

The two other bodies the walks stopped short of have their own archives: a
provider wrapping the call site is in slot_context_read_provided.txtar, and an
errorBoundary's @error handler in slot_context_read_boundary.txtar.
-- read.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import app "sngl:app"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

context #theme("light")

component framed(caption component) ui {
    caption {
        text(value="(none)")
    }
}

component main ui {
    framed() {
        component caption {
            text(value=theme)
        }
    }
}

app.window {
    main
}
